  "result": "Perfect! All 127 tests pass. Here's a summary of the ID normalization implementation:\n\n## Customer ID Normalization\n\n### Changes Made:\n\n**1. Added normalizeId() function (src/customers.js)**\n- Exported `normalizeId(id)` function that:\n  - Trims whitespace with `.trim()`\n  - Converts to uppercase with `.toUpperCase()`\n  - Handles any input type by converting to string first\n- Example: `normalizeId(' c-007 ')` \u2192 `'C-007'`\n\n**2. Updated getCustomer() (src/customers.js)**\n- Now normalizes the ID before looking up in the customer database\n- Allows any variation of customer ID format to work seamlessly\n\n**3. Updated createInvoice() (src/invoices.js)**\n- Normalizes customerId at the beginning of the function\n- Uses normalized ID for all validation and lookups\n- **Stores the normalized ID in the invoice object** (important for consistency)\n- Example: Creating an invoice with `c-002 ` stores `C-002` in the invoice\n\n**4. Test Coverage (8 new tests)**\n- `normalizeId` trims whitespace variations\n- `normalizeId` converts to uppercase\n- `normalizeId` is idempotent\n- `createInvoice` accepts lowercase customer IDs\n- `createInvoice` accepts mixed case IDs with spaces\n- `createInvoice` stores normalized customer ID\n- Various customer ID formats all work and resolve correctly\n\n### Key Decision:\n- **All customer IDs are normalized consistently** \u2014 this ensures that whether data comes in as `'c-001'`, `'C-001'`, `' c-001 '`, or any other variation, the system treats them as the same customer and stores them in canonical form (`'C-001'`)\n\n**All 127 tests passing** \u2713",
